1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,510
Every answer ChatGPT gives

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:03,330
you is written one

3
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:04,260
piece at a time,

4
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:05,360
and each piece

5
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:05,970
is a guess.

6
00:00:06,268 --> 00:00:06,868
So how does a

7
00:00:06,718 --> 00:00:07,998
guess sound so smart?

8
00:00:08,369 --> 00:00:09,469
First, tokens.

9
00:00:09,772 --> 00:00:10,572
Your message is

10
00:00:10,422 --> 00:00:11,782
chopped into tokens,

11
00:00:11,882 --> 00:00:13,332
small chunks of text.

12
00:00:13,639 --> 00:00:14,799
A token is about

13
00:00:14,649 --> 00:00:16,209
three-quarters of a word.

14
00:00:16,512 --> 00:00:16,982
Next,

15
00:00:16,832 --> 00:00:18,592
every token becomes a vector,

16
00:00:18,732 --> 00:00:20,182
a long list of numbers.

17
00:00:20,470 --> 00:00:22,030
In GPT-3,

18
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:23,020
that list is

19
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:25,510
12,288 numbers long.

20
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:26,879
Then, attention.

21
00:00:27,181 --> 00:00:28,721
Each token looks back at

22
00:00:28,571 --> 00:00:30,481
every token before it and

23
00:00:30,331 --> 00:00:31,841
decides which ones matter.

24
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:33,100
In the river bank,

25
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:34,760
bank pays attention to river,

26
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:35,320
not money.

27
00:00:35,586 --> 00:00:36,516
And that happens

28
00:00:36,366 --> 00:00:37,846
in 96 layers,

29
00:00:38,046 --> 00:00:39,056
one after another.

30
00:00:39,349 --> 00:00:40,119
At the end,

31
00:00:39,969 --> 00:00:41,459
the model scores every

32
00:00:41,309 --> 00:00:42,549
token it knows,

33
00:00:42,779 --> 00:00:45,579
all 50,257 of them,

34
00:00:45,769 --> 00:00:46,389
and picks a

35
00:00:46,239 --> 00:00:47,449
likely next one.

36
00:00:47,736 --> 00:00:49,126
Then it adds that token

37
00:00:48,976 --> 00:00:50,786
to your message and runs

38
00:00:50,636 --> 00:00:51,726
the whole thing again.

39
00:00:52,065 --> 00:00:53,525
For every single word.

40
00:00:53,819 --> 00:00:54,579
So where do the

41
00:00:54,429 --> 00:00:55,509
numbers come from?

42
00:00:55,808 --> 00:00:59,458
GPT-3 read 300 billion tokens

43
00:00:59,618 --> 00:01:00,848
and learned by guessing

44
00:01:00,698 --> 00:01:01,508
the next one,

45
00:01:01,828 --> 00:01:04,928
nudging 175 billion weights to make

46
00:01:04,778 --> 00:01:06,328
the right guess more likely.

47
00:01:06,612 --> 00:01:07,962
The idea came from an

48
00:01:07,812 --> 00:01:10,562
eight-author Google paper in 2017:

49
00:01:10,842 --> 00:01:12,662
Attention Is All You Need.

50
00:01:12,963 --> 00:01:13,813
So here's how

51
00:01:13,663 --> 00:01:14,323
to use it.

52
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,270
It predicts what sounds right,

53
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:17,280
not what is right.

54
00:01:17,573 --> 00:01:18,763
So give it the facts,

55
00:01:18,863 --> 00:01:20,093
show it an example,

56
00:01:19,943 --> 00:01:20,643
and ask it to

57
00:01:20,493 --> 00:01:21,413
check its work.

58
00:01:21,763 --> 00:01:23,153
Your chatbot is quietly

59
00:01:23,003 --> 00:01:24,373
guessing the next token,

60
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:26,383
over and over, really well.

61
00:01:26,692 --> 00:01:28,302
Follow for more breakdowns,

62
00:01:28,422 --> 00:01:29,272
and send this to

63
00:01:29,122 --> 00:01:30,512
the friend who thinks

64
00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:31,752
the AI is alive.

